    "answer": {
      "short": "The domain may have separate pieces. For example, let $f(x)=0$ for $x<0$ and $f(x)=5$ for $x>0$. Then $f'(x)=0$ throughout its domain and $f(2)=5$, but $f(-1)=0$.",
      "worked": "Take the domain $\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0\\}$ and define $f(x)=\\begin{cases}0,&x<0,\\\\5,&x>0.\\end{cases}$ On each part of its domain, $f$ is constant, so $f'(x)=0$ at every domain point. Yet $f(2)=5$ and $f(-1)=0$. A zero derivative does not force the same value across separate parts of a domain.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
